#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:10
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"Ever wondered what we mean by a specific term? We've been collecting the "
"most relevant terms below for your insight."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:12
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "<VPGlossary>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:19
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Admin\n"
": An admin(istrator) is a user in Zammad who has special rights and manages "
"your\n"
"  Zammad. An admin can, for example, configure user permissions, time "
"recording\n"
"  settings, automation and text modules.\n"
"  If you're looking for someone to adjust anything in your Zammad, your "
"admin\n"
"  is the right person to ask for.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:24
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Agent\n"
": An agent is a user in Zammad who works on tickets and responds to "
"customer\n"
"  inquiries. Agents can be admins too, meaning that they can change "
"settings,\n"
"  user permissions and so on (see above).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:30
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"API\n"
": An API (Application Programming Interface) is a set of rules and "
"protocols\n"
"  that allow different software applications to communicate with each "
"other.\n"
"  It allows you to connect third party systems with your Zammad "
"(e.g. social\n"
"  media, messengers, monitoring tools).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:34
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  You can learn more on our\n"
"  [API landing "
"page](https://zammad.com/en/product/features/rest-api){target=_blank}\n"
"  as well as in our [API reference](/en/reference/rest-api/intro).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:40
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Article\n"
": Each item within a ticket is called article. Ticket articles\n"
"  can be internal (only agents can see them) or public\n"
"  (customers can see them too; e.g. public notes or outgoing emails) and "
"include\n"
"  formatted text as well as attachments and inline images.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:48
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Automation\n"
": There are many processes that can be automated with Zammad.\n"
"  This means that certain actions take place automatically.\n"
"  An automation can be time-based or action-based and should cover every\n"
"  conceivable automation scenario.\n"
"  An example could be the recurring deletion of customers who are no "
"longer\n"
"  needed or the assignment of groups based on ticket attributes.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:54
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Autosave\n"
": Zammad's autosave feature makes sure that no work is lost. So if your "
"browser\n"
"  crashes, simply log in again and resume where you were before.\n"
"  Learn more on our\n"
"  [Autosave landing "
"page](https://zammad.com/en/product/features/autosave){target=_blank}.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:58
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Branding\n"
": Every company has a different identity. This is why Zammad lets you add "
"your\n"
"  logo, signature, links and other things!\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:62
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Changelog\n"
": With every new release comes a new changelog. It is basically a list of "
"all\n"
"  the things that have changed, from new enhancements to bug fixes.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:65
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  You can find them all on our\n"
"  "
"[GitHub](https://github.com/zammad/zammad/blob/stable/CHANGELOG.md){target=_blank}!\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:71
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Checkmk\n"
": Checkmk is a powerful IT monitoring tool that can send real-time status\n"
"  alerts to Zammad via email or REST API. Set these alerts up in Checkmk,\n"
"  and Zammad will automatically create, update and close tickets based on\n"
"  the health of your system.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:77
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Clearbit\n"
": Clearbit is a service designed to collect information on your\n"
"  contacts. Thus, new queries from unknown users in Zammad can be "
"automatically\n"
"  enriched with information such as company, number of employees,\n"
"  annual turnover, industry and much more.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:84
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Conflict Warning\n"
": When two agents edit the same ticket at the same time, a lot can go wrong "
"-\n"
"  from duplicate replies to overwritten messages. Zammad helps you to "
"avoid\n"
"  this with its integrated conflict warning. So if you see another agent's\n"
"  avatar and a little pen at the bottom of your ticket, it means the other "
"agent\n"
"  is currently editing it.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:88
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Content Sidebar\n"
": Right sidebar in the ticket view which includes the sidebar tabs and the\n"
"  active sidebar (if opened).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:94
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Core Workflows\n"
": This feature allows you to configure dynamic fields and ticket masks based "
"on\n"
"  attribute available in Zammad. For example you can disable or hide "
"fields,\n"
"  make them visible based on other fields or attributes, make them mandatory "
"and\n"
"  much more!\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:100
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"CTI\n"
": CTI stands for Computer Telephony Integration and allows you to collect\n"
"  detailed information on all your incoming and outgoing calls.\n"
"  This includes, for example, a call log, an overview of which agent is\n"
"  currently on a call, a caller ID search, and even a Do-Not-Disturb-Mode.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:109
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Custom Development (CD)\n"
": We are constantly working on improving Zammad, and we keep adding new\n"
"  features with every single release. However, sometimes our customers "
"might\n"
"  require a very specific new feature, addition, or adjustment that is "
"either\n"
"  very urgent or very particular to their individual use case.\n"
"  This is when a custom development can take place: We offer the customer "
"to\n"
"  develop the desired feature at a price that we agree upon previously\n"
"  (which is based on the expected hours needed for completion).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:117
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Customer\n"
": A customer is a person you communicate with via Zammad.\n"
"  For each customer there is a profile page showing all the tickets and "
"more\n"
"  information. Customers can be assigned to one or more organizations.\n"
"  Each customer can access their individual\n"
"  Customer Interface, where they see all their tickets with the current\n"
"  status and live updates.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:124
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Dashboard\n"
": The dashboard is the individual welcome page in Zammad and gives\n"
"  you feedback about your work.\n"
"  Here you can find all sorts of overviews, such as the open tickets,\n"
"  the average waiting time, or the reopening rate. You can also see what "
"your\n"
"  colleagues are doing by checking the Activity Stream.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:129
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Dialog\n"
": A dialog is a element which pops up in the middle of the screen because of "
"its\n"
"  importance. An example could be the dialog which asks you to save or "
"discard\n"
"  your unsaved changes.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:133
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Documentation\n"
": You probably have noticed already: you are reading a part of Zammad's\n"
"  documentation. 😉\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:139
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Elasticsearch\n"
": Zammad offers an Elasticsearch integration (a free and open search "
"engine)\n"
"  that makes the search process within Zammad super fast and powerful. You\n"
"  can even connect a reporting tool like Grafana to your Elasticsearch to\n"
"  create powerful reports and statistics.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:144
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Escalation\n"
": An escalation is what happens after the deadline for a ticket has "
"passed. You\n"
"  can configure it within Zammad's SLA (Service Level Agreement) "
"feature. An\n"
"  escalated ticket is marked red in your taskbar and the overviews.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:150
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Exchange Integration\n"
": The Exchange integration allows you to sync your contacts from your\n"
"  Exchange address book to Zammad. Every time a contact is updated\n"
"  in Exchange, it will be reflected in Zammad. This gives you direct\n"
"  access to all your contacts in your Zammad.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:157
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"External Authentication\n"
": External authentication is an easy, one-click option for your users to "
"log\n"
"  into Zammad. It has various benefits: not only is it faster but it also\n"
"  means that your users will have to remember fewer passwords.\n"
"  Zammad currently supports more than ten login providers, such as "
"Facebook,\n"
"  GitHub, GitLab, Google, or Microsoft.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:162
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Feature\n"
": A feature is a specific capability or functionality that Zammad provides "
"to\n"
"  you such as our integrations, productivity tools or additional channels.\n"
"  We keep adding new features with every release.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:169
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Feature Request\n"
": You can let us know if you are missing a particular feature in Zammad.\n"
"  We collect all of your wishes in our community forum in the\n"
"  [feature request "
"category](https://community.zammad.org/c/stuff-you-like-zammad-to-have-feel-free-to-discuss-and-add-proposals/6/all){target=_blank}.\n"
"  If a request comes in regularly and we think that it would be a\n"
"  great addition, we'll put it on our roadmap and start working on it.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:174
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Feature Sponsoring\n"
": If an organization urgently requires one of the features on the list,\n"
"  they can fast-forward the development and put it on top of the list by\n"
"  sponsoring it, which means that they cover the costs for the "
"development.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:179
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"GitHub\n"
": GitHub is a service for the version management of software development\n"
"  projects. It uses Git, a software that tracks changes in file sets.\n"
"  Here at Zammad, we use it to maintain and enhance our code.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:183
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  As Zammad is an open-source project, many developers and tech-lovers from "
"all\n"
"  over the world contribute to it. GitHub is where we coordinate all of "
"this.\n"
"  Follow the link to find [our "
"repositories](https://github.com/zammad){target=_blank}.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:187
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  Besides our own repos, Zammad also has an integration for GitHub.\n"
"  Using it allows you to show all relevant information about your\n"
"  issues directly in a sidebar tab, such as status or assignees.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:191
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"GitLab\n"
": GitLab is similar to GitHub.\n"
"  Here at Zammad, we use it for our internal development.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:195
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  There is also an integration that allows users to connect GitLab to "
"Zammad\n"
"  so that all their issues and their corresponding changes are reflected "
"in\n"
"  Zammad's sidebar tab as well.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:200
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Grafana\n"
": Grafana is an open-source reporting tool. It Elasticsearch as data "
"source.\n"
"  Hosted customers with a Plus plan can use it to get detailed statistics "
"of\n"
"  their instance. Self hosting Zammad users can use Grafana as well.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:209
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Groups\n"
": Groups are a synonym for departments or processing groups.\n"
"  Incoming tickets can be assigned to groups.\n"
"  Within the group, an owner can be defined, who is then responsible for\n"
"  this ticket. The access to tickets is controlled via the roles. In a role "
"you\n"
"  can define which permission it has based on the different groups.\n"
"  The possible permissions are \"read\", \"create\", \"change\", "
"\"overview\" and\n"
"  \"full\" (or none of them).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:213
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  If you have worked with the OTRS system in the past, you might remember "
"the\n"
"  principle of \"queues\". The groups in Zammad are the same as the queues "
"in\n"
"  OTRS.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:219
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Icinga\n"
": Icinga is a monitoring system that monitors the availability of an\n"
"  organization's entire system infrastructure.\n"
"  It can be integrated into Zammad so that it triggers a ticket in case\n"
"  of a warning situation.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:227
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"i-doit\n"
": i-doit is a CMDB (Configuration Management Data Base).\n"
"  It helps you to keep an eye on every piece of the physical and digital\n"
"  infrastructure. A corresponding integration makes it possible to connect\n"
"  it to Zammad, where it adds a new tab to Zammad's ticket sidebar so\n"
"  you can link to existing i-doit objects for easy reference.\n"
"  It also allows you to create Zammad tickets in i-doit.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:231
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Issue-Tracking System\n"
": Issue trackers are usually systems that track processes on a technical "
"level.\n"
"  Two of the best-known examples are GitHub and GitLab.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:235
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  Zammad is also often referred to as an issue-tracking system.\n"
"  However, as a helpdesk, it focuses on communication at the customer\n"
"  level rather than the technical level.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:240
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Kibana <Badge type=\"info\" text=\"on-premise only\" />\n"
": Kibana is a browser-based open-source reporting tool that focuses on "
"data\n"
"  evaluation. It was developed by Elastic, which is why it is not a "
"surprise\n"
"  that it uses data from Elasticsearch for its analytics.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:243
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  Kibana can be integrated with Zammad, allowing for helpdesk data to be\n"
"  mapped in the reporting tool.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:248
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Knowledge Base\n"
": Think of a very extensive set of FAQs - that's exactly what the\n"
"  Zammad knowledge base is. It collects all important information:\n"
"  definitions, processes, how-tos, organizational charts, etc.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:253
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  Knowledge base articles can be either internal or external, so you can\n"
"  either show them to the world (good for information on your product or\n"
"  service, for example) or keep them for your team (e.g. for internal "
"processes\n"
"  or team info).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:258
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"LDAP\n"
": A Lightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP) helps provide information\n"
"  about your users within Zammad. Authentication of users against the LDAP\n"
"  and LDAP role mapping to Zammad roles are also possible.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:264
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Macro\n"
": A macro is a series of actions. By starting the macro, the actions are\n"
"  triggered and executed, so users don't have to work through each\n"
"  individual step separately. It saves an enormous amount of time and "
"ensures\n"
"  that no step is forgotten.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:267
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  You can define your own macros and perform actions based on all "
"available\n"
"  attributes in the ticket.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:272
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Mentions\n"
": Mentions are a Zammad feature that allows you to tag another agent in a\n"
"  ticket. Just type [[@]][[@]] and the name. The selected person will be "
"notified\n"
"  and will be watching the ticket from now on.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:278
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Migrator / Migration Wizard\n"
": If a company wants to switch from another helpdesk software to Zammad,\n"
"  they often have one concern: What about their existing data?\n"
"  That's why we have built our migration wizards that help with migrating "
"all\n"
"  data.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:283
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Monit\n"
": Monit is an open-source monitoring tool that relies on a simple setup "
"and\n"
"  a strong community. You can integrate it with Zammad - this way,\n"
"  a ticket is created every time you receive an email in Monit.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:287
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Navigation Bar\n"
": The navigation bar is the left sidebar in Zammad which contains navigation "
"tabs,\n"
"  the search, notifications and many more items, depending on your system.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:291
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Navigation Tab\n"
": One element of the navigation bar. You can have multiple tabs open and "
"quickly\n"
"  switch between them.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:296
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Nagios\n"
": Nagios is another monitoring tool that alerts IT teams when an incident\n"
"  happens. It can be integrated with Zammad so that a ticket is created in\n"
"  case of an alert.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:301
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"On-Premise\n"
": This term means that Zammad is not provided as a cloud service of the\n"
"  company Zammad, but runs on a server which is controlled by a user or\n"
"  customer of Zammad. Self hosting means the same.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:307
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Organization\n"
": An organization is a grouping of customers that operate within the same\n"
"  company or company group.\n"
"  A customer, whose organization is \"sharing\", has access to all tickets "
"of its\n"
"  organization.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:313
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Owner\n"
": The owner of a ticket is the person responsible for and working on it.\n"
"  The owner can be changed to another agent.\n"
"  In this case, it is recommended to leave a handover note in the ticket "
"so\n"
"  that the new owner knows what to do.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:319
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Parent/Child Relationship\n"
": If one ticket results in other subtasks (or additional correspondences),\n"
"  you can split it into several tickets. The main one will then be the "
"parent\n"
"  ticket and the tickets with related subtopics are children.\n"
"  By the way: in the same way, you can also merge two tickets into one.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:325
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Placetel\n"
": Placetel is a Cloud Telephone System that allows users to make phone "
"calls\n"
"  via VoIP. Use your regular phone number and call someone directly on "
"their\n"
"  mobile or landline while still having all communication in one place\n"
"  in Zammad.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:330
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  Integrating a Placetel account with Zammad provides users with a call "
"log,\n"
"  making the history of their correspondences even more accurate.\n"
"  Callers are identified directly by their caller ID - a key function that\n"
"  saves agents a lot of time when assigning callers.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:335
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Priority\n"
": Every ticket gets assigned a priority. By default, the priority is 2 "
"(normal).\n"
"  Additionally there are the priorities 1 (low) and 3 (high). You can even\n"
"  add more priorities to your Zammad.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:343
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Release\n"
": Every few months, we release a new version of Zammad which is called a\n"
"  release. Every release adds new features to our software. There are major "
"and\n"
"  minor releases:\n"
"  major releases (such as Zammad 1.0, 2.0, etc.) bring major changes.\n"
"  Minor releases (such as 1.1, 2.1, etc.) are released in the meantime and "
"bring\n"
"  smaller updates.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:347
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  In our [release notes](https://zammad.com/en/releases){target=_blank} you "
"can\n"
"  find what features are included in new versions and also important "
"information\n"
"  about what to consider before updating Zammad.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:351
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Role\n"
": Every user has an assigned role. By default there are three roles:\n"
"  admin, agent, and customer.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:358
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  _Admins_ have the most rights: they can define roles, permissions,\n"
"  and settings for the entire team and instance.\n"
"  _Agents_ can view and edit tickets, but not change any settings other "
"than\n"
"  those of their own profiles.\n"
"  _Customers_ can view their ticket's processing status in their\n"
"  customer interface.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:360
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "  You can even add new roles to your Zammad.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:364
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Second Level Navigation\n"
": Refers to an additional menu next to the navigation (e.g. when opening "
"profile\n"
"  settings).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:368
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Side(Panel)\n"
": This is the panel which is flying in on the right side for actions like "
"ticket\n"
"  linking or ticket merging.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:373
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Sidebar Tab\n"
": In the content sidebar, there are different tabs like \"Customer\",\n"
"  \"Organization\" and \"Ticket\", depending on your system and "
"ticket. These are\n"
"  called sidebar tabs.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:375
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "  An active sidebar tab is the currently opened sidebar tab.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:381
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Sipgate\n"
": Sipgate is a SaaS solution for internet telephony.\n"
"  The Zammad integration for Sipgate provides users with a detailed call\n"
"  overview. If you have a customer that wants to get connected to a "
"certain\n"
"  agent, the caller log will tell you if this colleague is currently "
"available.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:388
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"SLA\n"
": A Service Level Agreement (SLA) is a contract between an end-user and a\n"
"  company that defines the minimum expected service requirements including\n"
"  quality, availability, and punctuality.\n"
"  They are used to set expectations and hold companies accountable for "
"keeping\n"
"  their promises.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:392
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  You can easily set up SLAs in Zammad and define parameters such as the "
"time\n"
"  for the first response, an update, and a solution. Once the deadline has "
"been\n"
"  reached, the ticket will escalate.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:398
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"S/MIME\n"
": S/MIME is the most widely-supported method for secure email "
"communication.\n"
"  By activating and configuring this integration in Zammad, outgoing "
"messages\n"
"  can be signed and encrypted and incoming messages can be decrypted and "
"their\n"
"  signature checked.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:403
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"SSO\n"
": Single-sign-on (SSO) allows you to access all your systems and devices "
"with\n"
"  just one login. There are various providers that make this process easy "
"and\n"
"  secure. Zammad currently supports SSO via different providers.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:410
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"State\n"
": Every ticket has a state which reflects the current progress of resolving "
"the\n"
"  issue. The state of a ticket can be changed manually or automatically "
"based\n"
"  on automation or SLA. Each state has a state types. There are four state "
"types\n"
"  by default and they are color-coded. You can even add additional states "
"to\n"
"  your Zammad.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:418
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Tags\n"
": Tags help you to categorize tickets. You can define them based on your "
"use\n"
"  case. For example, if you're a retail business, your tags could be based "
"on\n"
"  your product categories to help you organize tickets by the type of "
"product\n"
"  they refer to.\n"
"  But they could also be based on the type of request, e.g. refund, "
"delivery\n"
"  issue, complaint.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:421
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  Tags can be queried in conditions in an automation and assigned\n"
"  automatically, for example depending on keywords in the ticket title.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:429
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Text Module\n"
": If you find that you send the same answers over and over again,\n"
"  you can save yourself a lot of work and create a text module.\n"
"  This way, you just need to type the [[:]][[:]] shortcut in an article and "
"all\n"
"  available text modules are shown from which you can select the desired "
"one.\n"
"  You can limit the search results by adding parts of the text module's "
"name,\n"
"  keywords or content after the [[:]][[:]].\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:432
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  For example, here at Zammad, we have a text module with the shortcut\n"
"  `::ilff`, which turns into `I look forward to your feedback`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:437
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"(Ticket) Template\n"
": If you create many similar tickets, you can create a template for "
"them. This\n"
"  is helpful for introductions to your product/service or for drawing up "
"an\n"
"  offer.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:443
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"User\n"
": A user is any user of the ticket system. Each user has assigned\n"
"  permissions, which allow them to access certain areas and information.\n"
"  Users can have various roles, with the standard options being agent, "
"admin,\n"
"  and customer.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:449
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Webhooks\n"
": Webhooks are an easy way for systems to communicate with each\n"
"  other and allow you to send data to any other application.\n"
"  They allow you to inform a third party system about new information in "
"Zammad\n"
"  (e.g. sending a notification for new and updated tickets to your "
"Mattermost).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:452
#, markdown-text
msgid "Zammad : Zammad is the greatest helpdesk in the world. Period."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/glossary.md:453
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "</VPGlossary>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title #
#: src/en/reference/graphql.md:1 src/en/reference/graphql.md:6
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "GraphQL API"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/graphql.md:12
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"In addition to Zammad's [REST API](rest-api/intro), you can fetch, "
"manipulate and create data via the powerful and open-source [GraphQL "
"API](https://graphql.org/) too."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/graphql.md:16
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"This documentation isn't intended to cover everything about GraphQL. It "
"should give you a basic understanding about how you can fetch and "
"create/manipulate data to build upon. For a comprehensive guide, have a look "
"at [GraphQL's documentation](https://graphql.org/learn/)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/graphql.md:20
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"GraphQL is used by many web services, even big ones. It became some kind of "
"industry standard because of its efficiency and features. You can precisely "
"request the data you require which minimizes unnecessary data transfers and "
"searching the needle in a haystack."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/reference/graphql.md:23
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"Fetching the Zammad GraphQL schema (called introspection) enables "
"autocomplete and client-side validation while writing requests."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: src/en/reference/graphql.md:24 src/en/tutorials/sso-kerberos.md:63
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Getting Started"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
