#. type: Title #
#: src/en/tutorials/connect-config-elasticsearch.md:1
#: src/en/tutorials/connect-config-elasticsearch.md:6
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Connect and Configure Elasticsearch"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/tutorials/connect-config-elasticsearch.md:11
#, markdown-text
msgid "This guide shows you how to connect Zammad with Elasticsearch."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: src/en/tutorials/connect-config-elasticsearch.md:12
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Connect Elasticsearch with Zammad"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: src/en/tutorials/connect-config-elasticsearch.md:14
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Set the Elasticsearch URL"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/tutorials/connect-config-elasticsearch.md:17
#, markdown-text
msgid "Set the Elasticsearch server address; adapt it to your scenario."
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (sh)
#: src/en/tutorials/connect-config-elasticsearch.md:18
#, no-wrap
msgid "zammad run rails r \"Setting.set('es_url', 'https://localhost:9200')\"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: src/en/tutorials/connect-config-elasticsearch.md:22
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Set the Elasticsearch User and Password"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (sh)
#: src/en/tutorials/connect-config-elasticsearch.md:24
#, no-wrap
msgid "zammad run rails r \"Setting.set('es_user', 'elastic')\"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/tutorials/connect-config-elasticsearch.md:30
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Replace `<password>` with the one you got during the installation of "
"Elasticsearch. In case you need to create a new\n"
"password, run `/usr/share/elasticsearch/bin/elasticsearch-reset-password -u "
"elastic`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (sh)
#: src/en/tutorials/connect-config-elasticsearch.md:31
#, no-wrap
msgid "zammad run rails r \"Setting.set('es_password', '<password>')\"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: src/en/tutorials/connect-config-elasticsearch.md:35
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Add Certificate to Zammad"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#: src/en/tutorials/connect-config-elasticsearch.md:37
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Add it via Rails Console"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/tutorials/connect-config-elasticsearch.md:41
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"In case you are installing a new Zammad and didn't run through the getting "
"started wizard already, add the certificate via console:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (sh)
#: src/en/tutorials/connect-config-elasticsearch.md:42
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"sudo cat /etc/elasticsearch/certs/http_ca.crt | zammad run rails r "
"\"SSLCertificate.create!(certificate: STDIN.read)\"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#: src/en/tutorials/connect-config-elasticsearch.md:46
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Add it via UI"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/tutorials/connect-config-elasticsearch.md:51
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In case you already have a running and configured Zammad, you can add the "
"certificate in Zammad's admin settings\n"
"(_Settings > Security > SSL Certificates_) as an alternative. To show and "
"copy the auto-generated certificate from\n"
"Elasticsearch, run:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (sh)
#: src/en/tutorials/connect-config-elasticsearch.md:52
#, no-wrap
msgid "sudo cat /etc/elasticsearch/certs/http_ca.crt\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/tutorials/connect-config-elasticsearch.md:58
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"To add it in Zammad, either upload the certificate file or paste the content "
"in the dialog. Make sure to copy/paste the delimiters (e.g. `-----BEGIN "
"CERTIFICATE-----`) too."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: src/en/tutorials/connect-config-elasticsearch.md:59
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Build/Rebuild the Searchindex"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: src/en/tutorials/connect-config-elasticsearch.md:73
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Optional Settings"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/tutorials/connect-config-elasticsearch.md:78
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"We collected some useful settings you may want to apply. For further "
"information please have a look at [Elastic's "
"documentation](https://www.elastic.co/guide/en/elasticsearch/reference/current/index.html){target=_blank}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: src/en/tutorials/connect-config-elasticsearch.md:79
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Index Namespacing"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/tutorials/connect-config-elasticsearch.md:83
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"Useful when connecting multiple services or Zammad instances to a single "
"Elasticsearch server (to prevent name collisions during indexing)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (sh)
#: src/en/tutorials/connect-config-elasticsearch.md:84
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"zammad run rails r \"Setting.set('es_index', Socket.gethostname.downcase + "
"'_zammad')\"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: src/en/tutorials/connect-config-elasticsearch.md:88
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "File-Attachment Indexing Rules"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/tutorials/connect-config-elasticsearch.md:93
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"Zammad supports searching in file attachments, which means Elasticsearch has "
"to index those, too. Limiting such indexing can help preserve system "
"resources."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/tutorials/connect-config-elasticsearch.md:95
#, markdown-text
msgid "Files with these extensions will not be indexed:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (sh)
#: src/en/tutorials/connect-config-elasticsearch.md:96
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"zammad run rails r \"Setting.set('es_attachment_ignore',\\\n"
"[ '.png', '.jpg', '.jpeg', '.mpeg', '.mpg', '.mov', '.bin', '.exe', '.box', "
"'.mbox' ] )\"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/tutorials/connect-config-elasticsearch.md:102
#, markdown-text
msgid "Files larger than this size (in MB) will not be indexed:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (sh)
#: src/en/tutorials/connect-config-elasticsearch.md:103
#, no-wrap
msgid "zammad run rails r \"Setting.set('es_attachment_max_size_in_mb', 50)\"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: src/en/tutorials/connect-config-elasticsearch.md:107
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Asciifold"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/tutorials/connect-config-elasticsearch.md:112
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"By default, the [Asciifold feature of "
"Elasticsearch](https://www.elastic.co/docs/reference/text-analysis/analysis-asciifolding-tokenfilter){target=_blank} "
"is enabled. This can be useful if you deal with text which includes "
"diacritics and/or umlauts."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/tutorials/connect-config-elasticsearch.md:114
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"In case you need a more exact search, you can turn it off via [Rails "
"console](/en/reference/rails-commands#disable-asciifold)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: src/en/tutorials/connect-config-elasticsearch.md:115
#: src/en/tutorials/sso-kerberos.md:553
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Troubleshooting"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced div block (tip)
#: src/en/tutorials/connect-config-elasticsearch.md:117
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Troubleshooting unsuccessful or issue not described?\n"
"\n"
"If you can't solve your issue using the provided troubleshooting steps\n"
"or can't find your particular issue described here, feel free to\n"
"[ask the community](https://community.zammad.org){target=_blank} for "
"technical\n"
"assistance.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: src/en/tutorials/connect-config-elasticsearch.md:126
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Data Missing From the Web-UI / Search Data Missing or Incomplete"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/tutorials/connect-config-elasticsearch.md:132
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"A commonly reported issue is data missing from the Web-UI. This could be "
"tickets, articles, users or anything else [indexed by "
"Elasticsearch](/en/reference/es-indexed-attributes)  and can be caused by "
"missing or incomplete indexes."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/tutorials/connect-config-elasticsearch.md:136
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"If you are experiencing this issue and installed Elasticsearch according to "
"our [installation guide](/en/tutorials/install-elasticsearch), please follow "
"these steps to make sure Elasticsearch is working correctly."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#: src/en/tutorials/connect-config-elasticsearch.md:137
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Step 1: Verify Elasticsearch is Running"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (sh)
#: src/en/tutorials/connect-config-elasticsearch.md:139
#, no-wrap
msgid "sudo systemctl status elasticsearch\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/tutorials/connect-config-elasticsearch.md:145
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"This should output something like the following, make sure it says `Active: "
"active (running)`:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (sh)
#: src/en/tutorials/connect-config-elasticsearch.md:146
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"● elasticsearch.service - Elasticsearch\n"
"   Loaded: loaded (/lib/systemd/system/elasticsearch.service; enabled; "
"vendor preset: enabled)\n"
"   Active: active (running) since Tue 2021-07-20 09:38:21 UTC; 1h 4min ago\n"
"   Docs: https://www.elastic.co\n"
"   Main PID: 1790 (java)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/tutorials/connect-config-elasticsearch.md:155
#, markdown-text
msgid "Otherwise, try restarting it and check again:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (sh)
#: src/en/tutorials/connect-config-elasticsearch.md:156
#, no-wrap
msgid "sudo systemctl restart elasticsearch\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced div block (warning)
#: src/en/tutorials/connect-config-elasticsearch.md:160
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"If this fails, your Elasticsearch installation is probably broken.\n"
"Try completely purging and reinstalling Elasticsearch according to\n"
"our [installation guide](/en/tutorials/install-elasticsearch).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#: src/en/tutorials/connect-config-elasticsearch.md:166
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Step 2: Verify Zammad can Access Elasticsearch and Rebuild the Indexes"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/tutorials/connect-config-elasticsearch.md:170
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"Force Zammad to drop and rebuild the Elasticsearch indexes, optionally with "
"a specified number of CPU cores to use for re-indexing (example `[8]`):"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/tutorials/connect-config-elasticsearch.md:176
#, markdown-text
msgid "This should start rebuilding the indexes and output its progress:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (sh)
#: src/en/tutorials/connect-config-elasticsearch.md:177
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Dropping indexes... done.\n"
"Deleting pipeline... done.\n"
"Creating indexes... done.\n"
"Creating pipeline... done.\n"
"Reloading data...\n"
"   - Chat::Session...\n"
"      done in 0 seconds.\n"
"   - Cti::Log...\n"
"      done in 0 seconds.\n"
"\n"
"[...]\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/tutorials/connect-config-elasticsearch.md:194
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"Depending on the system performance and amount of data, this can take a "
"while to complete. Please let this task finish completely and wait until it "
"drops you back to the console."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/tutorials/connect-config-elasticsearch.md:199
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"If this fails or throws an error, there might be something else wrong with "
"your installation. Make sure you followed the complete Elasticsearch set up "
"and integration procedure according to our [installation "
"guide](/en/tutorials/install-elasticsearch)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced div block (tip)
#: src/en/tutorials/connect-config-elasticsearch.md:200
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In many situations where you're not successful with above steps, you\n"
"may want to check Elasticsearch's log file:\n"
"`/var/log/elasticsearch/elasticsearch.log`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/tutorials/connect-config-elasticsearch.md:207
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"After completing these steps, you should have verified your Elasticsearch "
"installation is running and rebuilt the indexes."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title #
#: src/en/tutorials/docker-file-handling.md:1
#: src/en/tutorials/docker-file-handling.md:6
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Docker File Handling Examples"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/tutorials/docker-file-handling.md:10
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"If you're not sure how to handle the backup files and how to create the "
"`restore` directory in the Docker volume, you can find some examples below."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: src/en/tutorials/docker-file-handling.md:11
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Restore Inside the Same Stack"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/tutorials/docker-file-handling.md:14
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Requires:** console access to the zammad-backup container.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/tutorials/docker-file-handling.md:18
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"If you want to restore a backup from the same stack, you just have to create "
"the directory and copy/move the files into it. The following example starts "
"the **zammad-backup** container and copies _all_ .gz files from the backup "
"directory into the restore directory:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (sh)
#: src/en/tutorials/docker-file-handling.md:19
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"docker compose run --rm zammad-backup bash -c \"mkdir "
"/var/tmp/zammad/restore; cp /var/tmp/zammad/*.gz /var/tmp/zammad/restore "
"-v\"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/tutorials/docker-file-handling.md:24
#: src/en/tutorials/docker-file-handling.md:47
#, markdown-text
msgid "Now start the stack to execute the restore process."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: src/en/tutorials/docker-file-handling.md:25
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Restore from Another Installation"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/tutorials/docker-file-handling.md:28
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Requires:** console access to the host system and the zammad-backup "
"container.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/tutorials/docker-file-handling.md:31
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"To **obtain** your backup files from another Docker Compose deployment, one "
"way is to copy it to the host system with `docker compose cp`:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (sh)
#: src/en/tutorials/docker-file-handling.md:32
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"docker compose cp zammad-backup:/var/tmp/zammad/ "
"/path/to/your/host/directory/\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/tutorials/docker-file-handling.md:38
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"In case you are searching for your backup files from a package installation, "
"have a look at the [Backup & Restore "
"(Package)](/en/tutorials/backup-restore) section. You don't need a full dump "
"for restoring your backup."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/tutorials/docker-file-handling.md:41
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"To **restore** the backup, place your files in a folder called `restore` on "
"the host system. This folder is mounted temporarily to `/restore` in the "
"backup container. The directory then gets copied to the actual directory:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (sh)
#: src/en/tutorials/docker-file-handling.md:42
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"docker compose run --rm -v /path/to/your/host/directory:/restore "
"zammad-backup bash -c \"cp -rv /restore /var/tmp/zammad/\"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: src/en/tutorials/docker-file-handling.md:48
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Use a Web GUI"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/tutorials/docker-file-handling.md:51
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Requires:** console access to the host system or Portainer access with the "
"permission to deploy a container.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/tutorials/docker-file-handling.md:53
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"This can be useful if you use Portainer to deploy Zammad and have limited "
"access to the host system."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/tutorials/docker-file-handling.md:56
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"Our example uses the tool [filebrowser](https://filebrowser.org/), but any "
"similar tool should work too. If you'd like to use such a tool permanently, "
"make sure to provide additional volumes for persistence (e.g. for their "
"database)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced div block (info)
#: src/en/tutorials/docker-file-handling.md:57
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\n"
"The steps below cover the restore process by uploading files. To get your "
"backup files in the same way from another\n"
"stack, you can follow steps 1-4 below and simply map the **zammad-backup** "
"volume of your _old_ stack. Then you can\n"
"download the files, stop and remove the filebrowser container and redeploy "
"it, following the steps below.\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '1. '
#: src/en/tutorials/docker-file-handling.md:66
#, markdown-text
msgid "Deploy filebrowser"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced div block (tabs)
#: src/en/tutorials/docker-file-handling.md:67
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\n"
"   === Via console\n"
"\n"
"   Deploy the container and provide the volume of **zammad-backup** and\n"
"   a port under which you want to access the web UI:\n"
"\n"
"   ``` sh\n"
"   docker run -v zammad-docker-compose_zammad-backup:/srv -p 8089:80 "
"filebrowser/filebrowser\n"
"   ```\n"
"\n"
"   === Via Portainer\n"
"\n"
"   In your Portainer web UI, go to **Containers** in the left menu and\n"
"   click the `Add container` button.\n"
"\n"
"   Add the following information:\n"
"\n"
"   - Name: enter a name which is not already in use.\n"
"   - Image: `filebrowser/filebrowser`\n"
"   - Map additional port: choose a port and map it to port `80` in the "
"container.\n"
"   - Advanced container settings:\n"
"     - Switch to **Volumes** and click the `map additional volume` button.\n"
"     - Enter `/srv` in the container section and select the volume "
"containing `zammad-backup`\n"
"   - Finally, click on **Deploy the container**.\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '2. '
#: src/en/tutorials/docker-file-handling.md:101
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"After the container is started, go to the web interface by using the IP "
"address and the port you defined."
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '3. '
#: src/en/tutorials/docker-file-handling.md:101
#, markdown-text
msgid "Log in with the default credentials `admin` / `admin`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '4. '
#: src/en/tutorials/docker-file-handling.md:101
#, markdown-text
msgid "You should now see at least 2 .gz files including a timestamp."
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '5. '
#: src/en/tutorials/docker-file-handling.md:101
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"Create a `New folder` by using the button on the left side. Name it "
"**restore**."
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '6. '
#: src/en/tutorials/docker-file-handling.md:101
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"Enter this folder and upload your backup files (on the top right corner with "
"the up arrow). If the upload fails, it might be required to change the "
"permissions of the files (e.g. readable for everyone)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/tutorials/docker-file-handling.md:103
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"Now start the stack to execute the restore process. After that, you can "
"safely delete the renamed folder and stop the filebrowser."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title #
#: src/en/tutorials/firewall-selinux.md:1
#: src/en/tutorials/firewall-selinux.md:6
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Firewall & SELinux"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/tutorials/firewall-selinux.md:10
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"This is just a collection of snippets which might be useful for you. Feel "
"free to skip parts and/or adapt it to your needs."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: src/en/tutorials/firewall-selinux.md:11
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "SELinux"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced div block (info)
#: src/en/tutorials/firewall-selinux.md:13
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The following commands only work on Ubuntu, Debian and CentOS. If you use "
"a\n"
"different distribution, please have a look at their documentation.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (sh)
#: src/en/tutorials/firewall-selinux.md:18
#, no-wrap
msgid "sudo chcon -Rv --type=httpd_sys_content_t /opt/zammad/public/\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (sh)
#: src/en/tutorials/firewall-selinux.md:22
#, no-wrap
msgid "sudo setsebool httpd_can_network_connect on -P\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (sh)
#: src/en/tutorials/firewall-selinux.md:26
#, no-wrap
msgid "sudo semanage fcontext -a -t httpd_sys_content_t /opt/zammad/public/\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (sh)
#: src/en/tutorials/firewall-selinux.md:30
#, no-wrap
msgid "sudo restorecon -Rv /opt/zammad/public/\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (sh)
#: src/en/tutorials/firewall-selinux.md:34
#, no-wrap
msgid "sudo chmod -R a+r /opt/zammad/public/\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: src/en/tutorials/firewall-selinux.md:38
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Firewall"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/tutorials/firewall-selinux.md:43
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"Ensure to open ports `80` and `443` (TCP & UDP) beside of the ports you "
"need. Below you can find a few examples for different distributions. If you "
"are using a different distribution, please have a look at their "
"documentation."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/en/tutorials/firewall-selinux.md:46
#, markdown-text
msgid ""
"Please note that the examples below only cover the distribution’s default "
"firewall. It may not cover your case."
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced div block (tabs)
#: src/en/tutorials/firewall-selinux.md:47
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\n"
"===Ubuntu\n"
"\n"
"```sh\n"
"sudo ufw allow 80\n"
"```\n"
"\n"
"```sh\n"
"sudo ufw allow 443\n"
"```\n"
"\n"
"```sh\n"
"sudo ufw reload\n"
"```\n"
"\n"
"===Debian\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced div block (tabs|info)
#: src/en/tutorials/firewall-selinux.md:47
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"We’re covering `nftables` in this part - `iptables` is discouraged\n"
"starting from Debian 10 (Buster). Our example uses the `input` chain, "
"yours\n"
"may be a different one!\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced div block (tabs)
#: src/en/tutorials/firewall-selinux.md:47
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\n"
"Add the following lines to `/etc/nftables.conf` or your specific rule "
"file.\n"
"Ensure to add these lines to your input-chain.\n"
"\n"
"```sh\n"
"sudo tcp dport { http, https } accept\n"
"```\n"
"\n"
"```sh\n"
"sudo udp dport { http, https } accept\n"
"```\n"
"\n"
"The result can look like the following. Keep in mind that your environment\n"
"could require different / more rules.\n"
"\n"
"```sh\n"
"table inet filter {\n"
"   chain input {\n"
"      type filter hook input priority 0; policy drop;\n"
"      ct state established,related accept\n"
"      tcp dport ssh log accept\n"
"      tcp dport { http, https } accept\n"
"      udp dport { http, https } accept\n"
"   }\n"
"\n"
"   chain forward {\n"
"      type filter hook forward priority 0; policy accept;\n"
"   }\n"
"\n"
"   chain output {\n"
"      type filter hook output priority 0; policy accept;\n"
"   }\n"
"}\n"
"```\n"
"\n"
"To load the rules, run:\n"
"\n"
"```sh\n"
"sudo systemctl reload nftables\n"
"```\n"
"\n"
"===CenOS, RHEL, OpenSUSE, SLES\n"
"\n"
"```sh\n"
"sudo firewall-cmd --zone=public --add-service=http --permanent\n"
"```\n"
"\n"
"```sh\n"
"sudo firewall-cmd --zone=public --add-service=https --permanent\n"
"```\n"
"\n"
"```sh\n"
"sudo firewall-cmd --reload\n"
"```\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

